# The audit harness under .claude/ is versioned with the rules it audits, so
# the rule data and the way it gets attacked share one history. Everything else
# under .claude/ (plans, worktrees, local settings) stays out.
#
# `!.claude/` comes first on purpose: a global ignore (core.excludesFile) may
# exclude the `.claude` *directory* itself, and git prunes excluded directories
# during traversal — no negation below can reach inside one. Re-including the
# directory here (repo .gitignore outranks the global file) lets git descend;
# `.claude/*` then re-excludes its contents, and the three negations carve out
# the harness.
!.claude/
.claude/*
!.claude/agents/
!.claude/workflows/
/target
